BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Fall 2026 George Link Jr. Environmental Awareness Lecture
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261005T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261005T190000
DTSTAMP:20260925T145501Z
UID:dartmouth-82584@free-food-dartmouth
SEQUENCE:0
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Original event page(s):\nhttps://home.dartmouth.edu/events/eve
 nt?event=82584\nhttps://envs.dartmouth.edu/news/2026/10/fall-2026-george-l
 ink-jr-environmental-awareness-lecture\n\nDescription:\nJoin us on October
  5th at 5:30pm in the Top of the Hop for our Fall 2026 George Link Jr. Env
 ironmental Awareness Lecture with bestselling author\, Robert Macfarlane!\
 nFrom the Elwha to the Klamath: on the lives\, deaths and rights of rivers
 \nA contemporary de-damming movement is sweeping Europe and the United Sta
 tes. Last year 542 dams were removed across 23 European countries\, and 10
 0 dams came down across 30 US states\, reconnecting 4900 miles of waterway
 : more than in any previous year in history. New Hampshire is part of this
  shift\, with dams coming down on the Cocheco\, the Piscataquog and the Me
 rrimack\, among others. The de-damming movement has accelerated in tandem 
 with\, and often in partnership with\, the Rights of Nature movement\, whi
 ch argues for a categorical shift in perception and treatment (legal\, str
 uctural and cultural-imaginative) of ecosystems from resource to relative\
 , object to fellow subject and use value to intrinsic value –– and pro
 poses recognizing rivers always-already as the bearers of fundamental righ
 ts.\nThis 2026 George Link Jr Environmental Awareness Lecture by the Briti
 sh writer Robert Macfarlane will explore what flows from the recognition o
 f rivers as having lives\, deaths and rights. By means of story and report
 age as well as history\, it will traces some of the complex\, contested hi
 stories of de-damming and river-rights movements\, from the Elwha and Klam
 ath rivers in the Pacific Northwest\, the Mutehekau Shipu (Magpie River) i
 n Nitassinan (north-east Québec)\, the Río Los Cedros in Ecuador\, and t
 he resurgent 'river-guardianship' movement in England. It will also argue 
 for the vital roles of storytelling\, music and art (or what Arne Naess ca
 lled 'beautiful acts') in inspiring river-activism and\, by extension\, wi
 der global currents of change.\nThis event is sponsored by the Hopkins Cen
 ter for the Arts at Dartmouth\, the Department of Environmental Studies\, 
 and the Department of Anthropology.\nhttps://envs.dartmouth.edu/news/2026/
 10/fall-2026-george-link-jr-environmental-awareness-lecture\n\nSponsor / o
 rganizer: Anthropology Department\, Dartmouth Center for Career Design\, E
 nvironmental Studies\, Hopkins Center for the Arts\n\nAudience: Public\n\n
 Source: Dartmouth\n\nWhy this was included: Free Food category\n\nFood ava
 ilability is inferred from the source listing\; verify details before atte
 nding.
LAST-MODIFIED:20260925T145501Z
LOCATION:Top of the Hop\, Hopkins Center for the Arts
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
URL:https://home.dartmouth.edu/events/event?event=82584
X-MICROSOFT-CDO-BUSYSTATUS:FREE
END:VEVENT
